Cutting geometry, feed, speed and material removal (@mech-cutting-kinematics: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @mechanical-engineering, #source "../sources/Design-and-Manufacturing-II-MIT-2025.lingua#r_2FHEWYSEAHBY7X6030TW00F32E", #mechanical-engineering "Cutting-forces-power-tool-life-and-chatter.lingua#r_7TSJ2BAC8N00177EEJVXCZ93MQ") { r_0DCYBG02XM5WFM7NEA5G5AEFYQ
**Read:** [Design and Manufacturing II — MIT 2.008, 2025](../sources/Design-and-Manufacturing-II-MIT-2025.lingua#r_2FHEWYSEAHBY7X6030TW00F32E), lectures 5–7, deriving the stated cutting geometry and units. Its machining material connects to the prior shaft, force and thermal work.

Turning, drilling and milling combine relative tool motion with feed and depth. In turning, cutting speed at diameter D is πDn, with n in revolutions per time. Feed per revolution multiplied by n gives axial feed rate. A changing diameter changes surface speed at fixed rpm.

For D=40 mm, n=600 rpm and feed 0.1 mm/rev, surface speed is approximately 75.398 m/min and feed rate 60 mm/min. A 100 mm engaged traverse takes 100 s before approach, acceleration and tool changes. In a straight turning pass from 40 to 38 mm over 100 mm, removed volume is 3900π mm³, not the volume of a full 2 mm radial layer.

**Work:** Derive removed volume and average removal rate from geometry, then compare the small-depth approximation. Explain feed per tooth for milling and distinguish it from table feed. Check tool engagement, travel limits and the effects of tool radius and orientation before converting a drawing to a path.

**Return:** [Mechanical engineering and machine design](../Mechanical-engineering-and-machine-design.lingua#r_0017YZ63HZBZ4P4WD8DECVWE4R).
